<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mansa Musa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1312">1312</a>–<a href="/years/1337">1337</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> Musa was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> from 1312 to 1337, under whom the empire reached its greatest wealth and extent. His pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1324, during which he distributed vast quantities of gold, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> famous across the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He patronised Islamic learning and architecture, including at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mansa-musa.jpg" alt="Mansa Musa" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: attributed to Abraham Cresques · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catalan_Atlas_BNF_Sheet_6_Mansa_Musa_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
